INT. BARBERSHOP - TUMBLEWEED, TEXAS - DAY

One chair. One mirror. A cracked leather strop.

EZEKIEL MOSS, fifties, barber, snips at the air. In the chair, CLEMENTINE HATCH, forties, in a man's hat and a long duster, sits with a towel round her neck.

EZEKIEL
Just the ends?

CLEMENTINE
Just the ends.

Snip. Snip.

EZEKIEL
You passing through?

CLEMENTINE
Looking for someone.

EZEKIEL
Aren't we all.

CLEMENTINE
A man called Rafferty. Red beard. Left ear missing.

EZEKIEL
Don't know him.

CLEMENTINE
You'd remember the ear.

EZEKIEL
I would.

The bell on the door jingles. DEPUTY LUKE ARMITAGE, twenty-five, wide-eyed, steps in, hat in hand.

LUKE
Pardon me, Mr. Moss. The sheriff wants a word with the lady.

EZEKIEL
She's mid-trim.

LUKE
He said it was urgent.

CLEMENTINE
(not turning)
Tell him I'm busy.

LUKE
He said you'd say that.

CLEMENTINE
Then he knows the answer.

Luke shifts his weight. Ezekiel lowers the scissors.

EZEKIEL
Son, why don't you take a seat and wait your turn.

Luke sits. The clock ticks. The snipping resumes.
